1)whatever we were doing in kubernetes creating pods, replciasets, depolyments, we were doing it within a namespace 

this namespace is known as default namespace and it is created by kubernetes automatically when the cluster is first setup 

2) kubernetes creates a set of pods and services for its internal purpose, such as those required by networking solution, the DNS,etc. 
to isolate these from the user and prevent you from accidentally delting or modifying the servcies 
kubernetes creates them under another namespace created at cluster startup named kube system

3)a third namespace created by kubernetes automatically is called kube public.
this is where the resources that should be made available to all users are created

if your environment is small, then you can continue working in default namespace
for enterprise or production purposes you should consider use of namespaces

you can also create your own namespaces

if you want to use the same cluster for dev and prod environment, but at same time isolate the resources b/w dev and prod, you can create a diff namsepace for each environment
this way you can isolate them and you prvent when you accidentally modify prod when you want to modify prod 

each of these namespaces can have its own set of policies that define who can do what 

you can also assign quota of resource(cpu, ram, rom etc..) to each of these namespaces, that way each namespaces is gauranteed a certain amount and does not use more than its allowed limit

----------------------------

the resources within a namespace can each other directly by their names

namesapce1: have web-app, db(also have db-servcie for connecting), backend pods

dev(namespace): have web-app, db, backend pods

web-app can reach the db service(a kubernetes service) using hostname db service 
mysql.connect("db-service")

web-app pod in namespace1 can reach a servcie in another namespace as well, for this you must append the name of namespace to the service

mysql(db-service.dev.svc.cluster.local)

the DNS: resource_name.namespace_name.resource_type.cluster.local

resource_name : the resource you are trying to access
namespace: namespace where the resource exists
resource_type: type of resource (pod, service, etc..)
resource type eg: svc
cluster.local : kubernetes domain

eg: db-service.dev.svc.cluster.local

you are able to do this because when a service is created, as DNS entry is automatically in this format

cluster.local -> default domain of the kubernetes cluster
svc -> subdomain for service, it says the domain is for servcie
dev -> namespace where the resource resided
service_name -> service which the whoel domain is referring

